al outpost, increasing pressure on White’s position. The knight on e4 is hard to remove (no adjacent pawns can kick it immediately), and it eyes f2, which is a vulnerable point in White’s camp. Additionally, it avoids passivity and maximizes activity.\n\nReason:  \nN: f6-e4 develops a piece to a dominant square, increases coordination, and creates immediate tactical threats against White’s uncastled king and loose pieces. It improves Black’s initiative and restricts White’s options.\n\nAnswer: N: f6-e4"}
